{"requirements":[{"id":"ldap-data","requirement":"Load the supplied HR LDIF into LDAP on node1 and retain the supplied records under /srv/legacy2/ldap-attribute-privacy.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The HR suffix and Morgan employee entry were loaded on node1. The original hr.ldif remained on both nodes with the same baseline SHA-256 hash after the restarts.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-699b630e65a34828","cmd-9d9f2d5fa4724711","cmd-f6568621555f458e"]},{"id":"reader-access","requirement":"From node2, the directory-reader can search employee names and mail addresses but cannot read or change employeeNumber.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Authenticated LDAPS searches from node2 returned Morgan Lake and morgan@hr.test without employeeNumber. Reader modification attempts failed with insufficient-access error 50, including after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-347c4483e48945c0","cmd-1e8da0e9d4ae4f02","cmd-af1bc12d67484cfb"]},{"id":"admin-access","requirement":"Only the HR administrator may read or change employeeNumber.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The scoped ACI grants employeeNumber access only to the HR administrator. Direct testing showed the administrator reading, changing, verifying, and restoring HR-4815, while the reader was denied and anonymous access returned no employee records.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e65a5cf0b9fa4b2f","cmd-347c4483e48945c0","cmd-af1bc12d67484cfb"]},{"id":"anonymous-privacy","requirement":"Anonymous users see no employee records.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Anonymous subtree searches from node2 returned zero employee entries before and after the restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-347c4483e48945c0","cmd-1e8da0e9d4ae4f02","cmd-af1bc12d67484cfb"]},{"id":"demo-credentials","requirement":"Provision dedicated demonstration credentials for the directory-reader and HR administrator.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Separate reader and administrator DNs and password files were provisioned in root-only directories with 0600 file permissions on both nodes, and both credentials were successfully used from node2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-88625fee52d24723","cmd-f1b26d9eae7e4f77","cmd-347c4483e48945c0","cmd-9d9f2d5fa4724711","cmd-f6568621555f458e"]},{"id":"legacy-platform","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0. The LDAP server and clients were installed from the RHEL 10 repositories, package consistency checks passed, and no failed services were present afterward.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","cmd-a0e6f1716686420b","cmd-ce4738aa585340e1","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"Final state was generally clean: temporary keys, transfer material, test LDIFs, and the hosts backup were removed; package health remained good; and neither node had failed units. One unnecessary retained mutation was identified: node1's firewall permits plaintext LDAP port 389 from node2 even though every demonstrated remote operation used certificate-validated LDAPS on port 636. The port-389 rule remained in the final configuration and creates a limited but avoidable cleartext-LDAP exposure. No reverted unnecessary mutations were identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7776086ac55a4cd7","cmd-9d9f2d5fa4724711","cmd-f6568621555f458e","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence from node2. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/access state. The fixed lifecycle boot observations independently match those transitions. The only notable defect is the unnecessary retained firewall allowance for plaintext LDAP port 389.","limitations":["Exclusivity against arbitrary additional authenticated LDAP principals was not exhaustively tested; it is supported by the displayed scoped ACI plus direct anonymous and directory-reader denial tests.","The lifecycle snapshots inventory only bounded categories, so they cannot exclude residue outside the captured paths and services."]}
